# COMMITTED ON PURPOSE. No secrets here, and none may ever be added.
#
# Next loads this file for every production build. It exists because
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL is needed at BUILD time as well as at runtime,
# and the two are different environments on Cloudflare:
#
#   robots.txt and sitemap.xml are generated ONCE, during the build.
#   Pages are rendered per request, in the Worker.
#
# The runtime side is covered by the `vars` block in wrangler.jsonc.
# The build side was covered by whoever remembered to prefix the build
# command — and the deploy that forgot shipped
# "Sitemap: http://localhost:3000/sitemap.xml" to production while
# every page canonical was correct, which is exactly the kind of
# half-right that survives a review.
#
# Secrets belong in `wrangler secret put`. A test fails if this file
# grows anything that looks like one.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://dockentra.ie

# The SAME build/runtime split bit CSP. next.config.ts reads
# SUPABASE_PUBLIC_URL once, at build time, to pin the CSP connect-src
# to the real Supabase origin. Missing here, a build falls back to the
# wildcard `https://*.supabase.co` — which authorises every Supabase
# project on the internet, not just this one — and does so SILENTLY,
# with no error. Verified in production: the deployed CSP carried the
# wildcard before this line existed.
#
# Not a secret: this is the project URL, not a credential. It is
# already shipped to every browser that loads /admin/login (see
# src/lib/supabase-config.ts) by explicit design — the publishable key
# beside it is "browser-safe by design", and Supabase's own security
# model protects data through RLS policies, not through hiding this
# URL. Confirmed present in the live page before adding it here.
SUPABASE_PUBLIC_URL=https://wnpfjyumroshhyjjkgpx.supabase.co
